    <h2>What do Aikido classes in Gdynia look like?</h2>

    <p>
      Our <strong>Aikido classes in Gdynia</strong> combine traditional methodology with a practical, calm
      introduction. Training includes a warm-up, learning how to move, ukemi, basic techniques and paired
      work. This is not a hall oriented around sparring or sports rivalry. The priorities are technique,
      safety, regularity and development.
    </p>

    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step">
        <span class="step-number">1</span>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Arrive early</h4>
          <p>Ideally 10-15 minutes before class. That is enough to calmly walk in, change and learn the dojo rules.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <span class="step-number">2</span>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Train from zero</h4>
          <p>Beginners do not need to know anything. The first class is an introduction, not a fitness or knowledge test.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <span class="step-number">3</span>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Decide after class</h4>
          <p>After training you can ask about the further plan, fees and next steps - with no pressure and no obligations.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>Who are the classes for?</h2>

    <div class="benefits-list">
      <div class="benefit-item">
        <h4>For beginners</h4>
        <p>If you have never trained a martial art before, you can start from zero.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="benefit-item">
        <h4>For adults</h4>
        <p>The evening schedule and the calm pace of entry suit working people well.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="benefit-item">
        <h4>For people looking for self-defence without the sports framing</h4>
        <p>Aikido develops control, timing, balance and work under pressure, without competitions or a sparring-based training model.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="benefit-item">
        <h4>For people who want to train long-term</h4>
        <p>This is not a quick-fix programme. It is a practice you can develop for years.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

